  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Drier and mostly sunny weather into next week.\n\nThe weather will be pretty benign for the next few days and for the \nfirst half of next week. The cold front that passed through is now \nin the Atlantic but wind gusts 20-25 mph along and west of the Blue \nRidge are likely to continue for the rest of today (Saturday). It \nwill be cool today with highs in the upper 40s to the mid-60s but \ntemperatures should recover a little early next week. By Tuesday, \nhigh temperatures may be in the lower 60s to lower 70s which will be \na little warmer than average for this time of year. The low \ntemperatures will still be on the colder side, with lows in the \nlower 30s to lower 40s. Elevated areas can expect lows to be in the \n20s tonight and the next few nights. A decent pressure gradient over \nthe Mid-Atlantic will continue as a surface high persists to our \nwest and two propagating lows pass by to our north. This will result \nin cold wind chill values in the 20s and 30s region wide for at \nleast the next three nights (including tonight). By the middle of \nnext week, 500mb heights become more zonally oriented allowing for \nthe recover of temperatures. This \"warming\" period is brief as model \nguidance is pretty confident on a very large mid-level trough \nswooping as far southward as the Gulf Coast by Friday. This \nimpressive feature will likely be carrying a surface low which will \nbring a chance of precipitation to the area towards the end of next \nweek. The colder air that will be traveling with it means there is a \nchance of some upslope snow in the western mountains and widespread \nsub-freezing temperatures for most of the area. Still, this event is \none week away so, for now, all we have is a signal.",
